Found while reviewing #7125 by reading `WbConnector::snapNow()`. Confirmed with a numerical replica of the snapping code, not yet reproduced in a world file.

### 1. The z-axis of a static connector is rotated as if its body had moved

In [`snapNow()`](https://github.com/cyberbotics/webots/blob/ad6dceb4399455891da8762904e29c4b7d364810/src/webots/nodes/WbConnector.cpp#L388-L406), the z-axes are read from the matrices, which `snapXAxes()` doesn't update, so they are corrected by hand:

```cpp
rotateVector(qa, z1);
rotateVector(aq, z2);
```

This assumes both bodies were rotated: `b1` by `qa` and `b2` by its inverse. When one connector (or its parent) has no Physics node, `rotateBodies()` rotates only the dynamic body, by the full angle. The static connector's z-axis is still rotated anyway. `snapRotation()` then computes its axis `w = z1 × z2` from a wrong vector. `w` is no longer parallel to the x-axes, so the rotational snap partly undoes the x-axis alignment.

With the default tolerances, the x-axes of the locked connectors end up misaligned by up to about 11°. This happens whether the static connector starts the lock or the dynamic one does. It only shows up when the connectors also need rotating around their axis (`numberOfRotations` > 0). It does not show up when the misalignment is purely about the z-axis, as in #6200.

Suggested fix, which brings the misalignment down to floating-point precision in all cases:

```cpp
if (b1)
  rotateVector(qa, z1);
if (b2)
  rotateVector(aq, z2);
```

### 2. Uninitialised quaternion when the x-axes are already aligned

[`snapXAxes()`](https://github.com/cyberbotics/webots/blob/ad6dceb4399455891da8762904e29c4b7d364810/src/webots/nodes/WbConnector.cpp#L273-L274) returns early, without writing its output quaternion, when `x1 × x2` is exactly zero (`WbVector3::isNull()` tests for exact zero). `snapNow()` then uses the uninitialised `qa` to build `aq` and to rotate `z1` and `z2`. This is undefined behaviour. It is probably harmless whenever the stack holds zeros, because a zero quaternion leaves the vectors unchanged in `rotateVector()`.

Suggested fix: set `q` to `{1, 0, 0, 0}` before the early return in `snapXAxes()`, or initialise `qa` that way in `snapNow()`.
